Use case: stylized-concept
Asset type: animation sprite sheet for a vintage green phosphor radar display.
Primary request: exactly 16 animation frames in a strict 4 by 4 grid, read left to right then top to bottom. Each square cell contains the exact SAME circular radar screen, centered, identical size and locked registration, filling 94% of its square. Black background outside each circle. No gutters, no borders, no labels.
Style: classic early 1990s adventure-game detailed painterly pixel art, dark green CRT glass, subtle phosphor grain, fine dim green concentric range rings and crosshair. Straight-on orthographic view. Screen only, no console or metal bezel.
Animation: one complete clockwise revolution. Frame 1 beam points straight up at 12 o'clock; subsequent frames advance clockwise by exactly 22.5 degrees; frame 16 is at 337.5 degrees. A thin luminous lime-green leading edge and a soft narrow phosphor wedge trailing behind it COUNTERCLOCKWISE, fading across about 60 degrees. The rest of the scope is very dark. Range rings and crosshair remain identical, fixed, dim in every frame. No baked stationary beam.
Contacts: exactly four small stationary target blips, at 1:30 radius 55%, 4:30 radius 68%, 7:30 radius 48%, and 10:30 radius 72%. Every contact stays at precisely the same coordinate in all frames. A contact lights up brightly when the beam reaches its angle, then fades gradually over the next four frames and is almost invisible for the rest of the revolution. Small green point cores and soft halos, no symbols or text.
Constraints: all sixteen cells equal size, same circle center/radius/grid/contact registration; only sweep angle and contact brightness change. The last frame must transition naturally into the first. No perspective, no words, no watermark, no montage of different designs.
